First Author: Michelson
Title: Developing machine learning models to predict primary graft dysfunction after lung transplantation
Source number: 37  ￼

Very short summary: This single-center retrospective study developed machine-learning models to predict grade 3 primary graft dysfunction (PGD) within 72 hours after bilateral lung transplantation using donor and recipient variables known at donor-offer acceptance. Five key findings were: (1) 576 bilateral lung transplant recipients were analyzed; (2) 173 patients (30%) developed PGD grade 3; (3) after feature engineering, 100 variables were reduced by lasso regression to 11 pretransplant features; (4) among candidate models, K-nearest neighbors (KNN) was selected as the final model because it had the best calibration with acceptable performance and relatively narrow uncertainty; and (5) the final selected KNN model achieved an AUROC of 0.65. Figures 1–6 and Tables 1–4 were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

