	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The study used donor and recipient pretransplant structured data from UNOS, UNet, and the local EHR. Four prediction algorithms were trained: LR, KNN, XGB, and SVC. Feature reduction was done with lasso regression, and hyperparameters were optimized using 5-fold cross-validation with random grid search. Feature importance was assessed using SHAP. The cohort was split 75%/25% into train/test sets.  ￼

Section 4: AI Quality & Reproducibility (CONVINCE Standards)
	•	Missing Data Handling: Simple Imputation
	•	Class Imbalance Addressed: No
	•	Code Availability: Other
	•	Data Preprocessing / Normalization Described: Yes
	•	Training Size (N): 432
	•	Test Size (N): 144
	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: The paper states that “missing data were median imputed for continuous data and mode imputed for binary data,” which is simple imputation. Preprocessing included binary encoding of categorical variables and MinMax scaling fit on the training data and applied to the test data. The population was divided into a 75%/25% train-test split, which corresponds to about 432 training and 144 test patients from 576 total. I did not see explicit class-imbalance mitigation. The model was deployed on a secure website, but no source-code release was stated, so code availability is best classified as Other.  ￼

